**The hardware wars are splitting into factions.** Nvidia's stock [fell another 4%](https://exa.ai/library/markets/stock/NVDA?date=2026-02-28) on Friday to close at $177.30, extending a brutal week that started with a [5.5% post-earnings plunge](https://blockonomi.com/nvidia-stock-slides-5-after-strong-q4-earnings-beat/) despite record $68.1 billion in Q4 revenue and a $78 billion Q1 guide that [crushed consensus](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/nvidia-beats-2-billion-guides-150612639.html). The market's anxiety found a name: AMD landed a [$60 billion deal with Meta](https://x.com/stock_analysisx/status/2027506904633905357) to supply MI450 GPUs and custom CPUs over five years, with Meta receiving warrants and an option to [buy up to 10% of AMD](https://x.com/stock_analysisx/status/2027506904633905357). Jensen Huang is no longer selling to a monopsony — he is selling into a market that is actively hedging against him. Goldman Sachs projects hyperscaler AI capex will reach [$667 billion in 2026](https://news.futunn.com/en/post/69261531/goldman-sachs-on-ai-trading-ai-infrastructure-faces-risks-in), up 62% year-over-year. The spend is accelerating. The monopoly is not.

